body:
### Tested versions

Experienced in Godot 4.7.1 (a13da4f), on Gentoo Linux.

I am not sure if this is a regression, or the intended behavior of `Node::get_node_or_null()` being exhibited in an unanticipated edge-case.  I can do some regression testing soon if this is indeed a bug.

### System information

Godot v4.7.1.stable (a13da4feb) - 'Gentoo' '2.18' on Wayland - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 1 monitor - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated AMD Radeon RX 6700 XT (RADV NAVI22) - AMD Ryzen 9 7950X3D 16-Core Processor (32 threads) - 124.9 GiB memory - PulseAudio (44100 Hz, Stereo/mono)

### Issue description

`Node.duplicate()` / `Node::duplicate()` clones a branch of a scene tree and then copies properties from the original branch to the newly created clone.  In `Node::_duplicate_properties`, an effort is made to detect cases when properties refer to children of the current node, so these properties can be updated to point to the newly created copies.  This appears to be implemented for Variant::Object properties as well as Arrays.  The first example is repeated below.

[node.cpp:3097](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/4.7.1-stable/scene/main/node.cpp#L3097)

```c++
            if (value.get_type() == Variant::OBJECT) {
                Node *property_node = Object::cast_to<Node>(value);
                Variant out_value = value;
                if (property_node && (p_root == property_node || p_root->is_ancestor_of(property_node))) {
                    out_value = p_copy->get_node_or_null(p_original->get_path_to(property_node));
                }
                p_copy->set(name, out_value);
            }
```

In the case where `property_node` has a default automatically-generated name (e.g. `@Node@3`), `Node::get_node_or_null()` fails to traverse the path returned by `get_path_to()`, resulting in the duplicate Node's properties being set to null.  When the nodes in the scene tree being duplicated are given names explicitly (either by setting `node.name = "a_clever_name"` or by setting `force_readable_name` in `Node.add_child()`), the properties in the duplicated tree are valid.

### Steps to reproduce

A minimal test case can be produced with two scripts.  By switching which lines are commented in node_refs.gd, you can see the difference in behavior.  The same behavior can also be observed when calling `Node.duplicate()` with `DUPLICATE_INTERNAL_STATE` on properties which aren't given the `@export_storage` annotation.

```gdscript
# main.gd - attached to main scene to run the test
extends Node2D

func _ready() -> void:

	# Original node
	var original := NodeRefs.new()
	add_child(original)
	print("original.single_ref: %s" % original.single_ref)
	print("original.many_refs.size: %d" % original.many_refs.size())
	for n in original.many_refs:
		print("  %s - name: %s" % [n, n.name])

	# Duplicate node
	var clone = original.duplicate(DUPLICATE_DEFAULT & ~DUPLICATE_USE_INSTANTIATION)
	add_child(clone)
	print("clone.single_ref: %s" % clone.single_ref)
	print("clone.many_refs.size: %d" % clone.many_refs.size())
	for n in clone.many_refs:
		print("  %s - name: %s" % [n, n.name if n else "NONE"])

```

```gdscript
# node_refs.gd - an object to test duplication on
class_name NodeRefs
extends Node

const num_refs = 5

@export_storage var initialized :bool = false
@export_storage var single_ref :Node
@export_storage var many_refs :Array[Node]

func _ready() -> void:
	if not initialized:
		single_ref = Node.new()
		add_child(single_ref) # Breaks
		#add_child(single_ref, true) # Works

		for i in range(0, num_refs):
			var n := Node.new()
			add_child(n) # Breaks
			#add_child(n, true) # Works
			many_refs.append(n)

		initialized = true
```

Produces output:

```text
original.single_ref: @Node@3:<Node#36473669129>
original.many_refs.size: 5
  @Node@4:<Node#36490446346> - name: @Node@4
  @Node@5:<Node#36507223563> - name: @Node@5
  @Node@6:<Node#36524000780> - name: @Node@6
  @Node@7:<Node#36540777997> - name: @Node@7
  @Node@8:<Node#36557555214> - name: @Node@8
clone.single_ref: <Object#null>
clone.many_refs.size: 5
  <Object#null> - name: NONE
  <Object#null> - name: NONE
  <Object#null> - name: NONE
  <Object#null> - name: NONE
  <Object#null> - name: NONE
```



